<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-02">
  <title>Week 2: Outer Measure on $\mathbf{R}$ (2A) </title>

  <subsection xml:id="Motivation">
    <title>Motivation and Definition of Outer Measure</title>

    <p>
      Lebesgue's revolutionary new idea of how to integrate is that we should partition the range of the function and not the domain.
    </p>

    <p>
      This means that the integral will take the form of a sum
      <md>
         S = \sum \eta_i m(E_i)
      </md>
      that ranges over all output values $\eta_i$. In this sum, $E_i$ is the set of all $x$ in the domain such that $f(x) = \eta_i$.
    </p>

    <p>
      If $E_i$ is an interval, then $m(E_i)$ should denote the length of the interval. Similarly if $E_i$ is a finite union of intervals, then $m(E_i)$ should be the sum of the lengths of each sub-interval. 
    </p>

    <p>
      The set $E_i$ can be more complicated then an interval or a union of intervals. How should we define $m(E_i)$ in this case?
    </p>

    <p>
      Lebesgue's first attempt at generalizing the length of an interval led to the development of Outer Measure.
    </p>

    <p>
      To define Outer Measure we first define the length of an open interval.
    </p>

   <definition xml:id="def-length">
    <title>Length of an Open Interval</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The length $\ell(I)$ of an open interval is defined by 
        <md>
          \ell(I) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  b-a \amp \text{ if $I = (a,b)$ for some $a,b \in \mathbf{R}$ with $a &lt; b$,} \\
      \hfill  0  \amp \text{ if $I = \emptyset$,} \\
      \hfill  \infty \amp \text{ if $I = (-\infty,a)$ or $I = (a, \infty)$ for some $a \in \mathbf{R}$,} \\
      \hfill  \infty  \amp \text{ if $I = (-\infty, \infty)$} 
  \end{cases}
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
   </definition>

   <p>
    If $A \subseteq \mathbf{R}$ is an arbitrary set then the size of $A$ should be less than the lengths of a sequence of intervals that contain $A$. By taking the infinimum over all such covers of $A$ we get a pretty good estimate for the size of $A$.
   </p>

   <definition xml:id="def-outer-measure">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The outer measure of $A \subseteq \mathbf{R}$ is denoted by $|A|$ and defined by
        <md>
          |A| = \inf \left\{ \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \ell(I_k): I_1, I_2, \ldots \text{ are open intervals such that } A \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty} I_k \right\}
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
   </definition>

   <example>
    The outer measure of the closed interval $[a,b]$ is $b-a$.
   </example>

   <proof>
    <p>
       First we will show that $|[a,b]| \leq b-a$. Let $\epsilon>0$ be arbitrary. Then $[a,b]$ is contained in the open interval $(a-\epsilon, b+\epsilon)$, which has length $b-a+2\epsilon$. Since $\epsilon$ can be made arbitrarily small, it follows that $|[a,b]| \leq b-a$.
    </p>

    <p>
      To prove the other direction we will need the Heine-Borel theorem.
    </p>
   </proof>

   <theorem xml:id="thm-heine-borel">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let $F \subseteq \mathbf{R}$. The following are equivalent.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              $F$ is compact. (That is, every sequence of points in $F$ has a subsequence that converges in $F$.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              $F$ is closed and bounded.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Every open cover of $F$ contains a finite subcover.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
   </theorem>
   
   <p>
    We now return to the proof that $|[a,b]| \geq b-a$, suppose that $[a,b] \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty} I_k$.
   </p>

   <proof>
    <p>
      By the Heine-Borel Theorem, there is a finite collection of these sets so that $[a,b] \subseteq I_1 \cup I_2 \cup \ldots \cup I_n$.
    </p>
    <p>
      We will prove by induction that
      <md>
        \sum_{k=1}^n \ell(I_k) \geq b-a
      </md>
      which implies that $|[a,b]| \geq b-a$.
    </p>

    <p>
       For the base case, note that if $n = 1$ then this means that $[a,b] \subseteq I_1$ and hence $\ell(I_1) \geq b-a$.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now we assume that $n &gt; 1$ and $[a,b] \subseteq I_1 \cup I_2 \cup \ldots \cup I_n$ implies that 
      <md>
        \sum_{k=1}^n \ell(I_k) \geq b-a
      </md>
      for all choices of $a,b \in \mathbf{R}$ with $a &lt; b$. 
    </p>

    <p>
      Now suppose that $I_1, I_2, \ldots I_{n+1}$ are open intervals and $[a,b] \subseteq I_1 \cup I_2 \cup  \ldots \cup I_{n+1}$. Then $b$ belongs to one of those intervals and by relabeling them if necessary we can assume that $b \in I_{n+1}$.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose $I_{n+1} = (c,d)$. If $c \leq a$ then $a \in I_{n+1}$ and $\ell(I_{n+1}) \geq b-a$. Hence we have proven what we want if this is the case.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus we can assume that $a &lt; c &lt; b &lt; d$.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since $I_{n+1}$ only covers $(c,d)$, this means that $(a,c) \subseteq I_1 \cup I_2 \cup \ldots \cup I_n$. Hence by the induction hypothesis,
      <md>
         \sum_{k=1}^n \ell(I_k) \geq c-a.
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus,
      <md>
        <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^{n+1} \ell(I_k) = \sum_{k=1}^n \ell(I_k) + \ell(I_{n+1}) \amp \geq c-a + b-c </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =b-a,</mrow>
      </md>
      which completes the proof. \qed
    </p>

   </proof>


  </subsection>


